    steps: [
      { text: "Two questions on every request: who are you (authentication), and may you do this (authorization). Identities live outside RBAC; Roles say what is allowed; bindings connect the two. Press Next." },
      { text: "Humans are authenticated externally. Kubernetes has no User objects: a client certificate's CN becomes the username and its O fields the groups, or an OIDC `id_token` is checked against the issuer's public keys.", on: ["human", "authn"], flow: ["e-human"] },
      { text: "Workloads use ServiceAccounts: namespaced API objects that are identities inside the cluster. Every namespace gets one named `default`; a Pod uses it unless `spec.serviceAccountName` says otherwise.", on: ["sa", "pod"] },
      { text: "The kubelet gets a short-lived token for the Pod's ServiceAccount through the TokenRequest API and mounts it as a projected volume. It rotates the token before expiry; deleting the Pod invalidates it.", on: ["kubelet", "pod"], flow: ["e-mount"] },
      { text: "The app sends the token as a bearer token. The API server authenticates it as `system:serviceaccount:shop:billing`, in the groups `system:serviceaccounts` and `system:serviceaccounts:shop`.", on: ["pod", "authn"], flow: ["e-pod"] },
      { text: "Authorization is deny by default. RBAC has no deny rules: permissions are purely additive, so a request is allowed if any binding grants that verb on that resource. Otherwise: 403 Forbidden.", on: ["authz", "rb", "crb"], flow: ["e-a1", "e-check"] },
      { text: "A Role lists rules (API groups, resources, verbs) in one namespace. A RoleBinding grants it to subjects: users, groups or ServiceAccounts. A binding's `roleRef` can't be changed; you delete and recreate the binding.", on: ["rb", "sa", "role"], flow: ["e-rb-sa", "e-rb-role"] },
      { text: "A RoleBinding can also reference a ClusterRole. The permissions then apply only inside the RoleBinding's namespace. This is how you reuse the built-in `view`, `edit` and `admin` roles per namespace.", on: ["rb", "cr"], flow: ["e-rb-cr"] },
      { text: "A ClusterRoleBinding grants a ClusterRole everywhere: every namespace, plus any cluster-scoped resources the role covers, such as nodes. Here the group `sre` gets `view` in every namespace. `view` still excludes Secrets and has no rules for nodes.", on: ["crb", "grp", "cr"], flow: ["e-crb-grp", "e-crb-cr"] },
      { text: "Verbs map to HTTP: `get`, `list`, `watch`, `create`, `update`, `patch`, `delete`, `deletecollection`. Subresources are named like `pods/log` or `pods/exec`. Note: `list` on Secrets returns their data too.", on: ["role", "cr"] },
      { text: "To test, ask the API server: `kubectl auth can-i list pods -n shop --as=system:serviceaccount:shop:billing` answers yes or no; `kubectl auth can-i --list -n shop` shows everything you can do.", on: ["human", "authz"], flow: ["e-human", "e-a1"] },
      { text: "After authorization, admission. Pod Security Admission reads namespace labels like `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted`. Levels: `privileged`, `baseline`, `restricted`. `enforce` rejects Pods; `warn` and `audit` only report.", on: ["adm", "g-ns"], flow: ["e-a2"] },
    ],
    say30: "Authentication says who you are. Humans come from outside: client certificates or OIDC tokens, since Kubernetes has no user objects. Workloads use ServiceAccounts; the kubelet mounts a short-lived, auto-rotated token into the Pod. Then RBAC authorizes. It's deny by default and purely additive. A Role or ClusterRole lists verbs on resources; a RoleBinding grants it within one namespace, and a ClusterRoleBinding grants a ClusterRole cluster-wide. A RoleBinding can point at a ClusterRole to reuse it in one namespace. I check with `kubectl auth can-i`, using `--as` for a ServiceAccount. Finally Pod Security Admission enforces privileged, baseline or restricted per namespace, set by labels.",
    gotchas: [
      "There's no deny rule in RBAC. To take access away you remove a binding or narrow a role; you can't override a grant from another binding.",
      "`get`, `list` and `watch` all return full objects. Granting `list` on Secrets is the same as granting read access to every Secret's data.",
      "A RoleBinding to a ClusterRole is namespace-scoped. Only a ClusterRoleBinding grants across all namespaces or on cluster-scoped resources like nodes.",
      "Pod Security `enforce` applies to Pods, not Deployments. The Deployment is accepted, and its ReplicaSet then fails to create Pods; look at the ReplicaSet's events. Set `warn` to the same level to get a warning at apply time.",
    ],
    atScale: [
      "Hundreds of teams: grant per namespace with RoleBindings, not ClusterRoleBindings. Avoid wildcards, which also cover CRDs added later, and keep everyone out of `system:masters`, which bypasses RBAC and can't be revoked by removing bindings.",
      "Admission webhooks sit on the write path of every matching request. Mutating webhooks run one after another, validating ones in parallel. The timeout defaults to 10 seconds (allowed 1 to 30); set it small and scope calls with `namespaceSelector`, `objectSelector` and `matchConditions`. The project prefers CEL-based admission policies where they fit.",
      "`failurePolicy` defaults to `Fail`, so a webhook outage rejects every matching write. Run webhooks HA behind a Service, don't match `kube-system` or node Leases, and watch for two webhooks that each need the other's Pods to start.",
      "One noisy client must not starve the rest. API Priority and Fairness splits the API server's concurrency (`--max-requests-inflight` 400 plus `--max-mutating-requests-inflight` 200 by default) across priority levels, uses `ByUser` or `ByNamespace` flows and shuffle sharding, and counts rejections in `apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total`.",
      "In multi-tenant clusters, create rights alone can exhaust etcd by object size or count. Cap object counts per namespace with ResourceQuota, and review bindings periodically: a new user with a deleted user's name inherits their bindings.",
    ],
    agentic: [
      "Agent identity is the core control. Run each agent as its own ServiceAccount or OIDC identity with a namespaced Role, never your kubeconfig or `cluster-admin`, so `kubectl auth can-i --list --as=system:serviceaccount:<ns>:<agent>` shows its reach and audit logs name it.",
      "RBAC can grant `pods/exec` but cannot limit which commands run inside it, as the Kubernetes blog on production debugging notes. Keep exec, `escalate`, `bind`, `impersonate` and Secret reads out of agent roles and send writes through a human approval step.",
      "Tool-level guards are defence in depth, not the boundary. kubectl-ai asks before resource-modifying commands only while `skipPermissions` is false, and the EKS MCP Server (preview) separates read-only from write tools; under prompt injection, the RBAC on the identity is what holds.",
    ],
    quiz: [
      { q: "You bind the ClusterRole `edit` to a user with a RoleBinding in namespace `dev`. What can they edit?", a: "Only objects in `dev`. A RoleBinding grants the referenced ClusterRole's permissions within its own namespace." },
      { q: "How does a Pod authenticate to the API server, and as whom?", a: "With the projected ServiceAccount token the kubelet mounted, sent as a bearer token. It authenticates as `system:serviceaccount:<namespace>:<name>`." },
      { q: "A Deployment applied fine but no Pods appear in a namespace labelled `enforce: restricted`. Why?", a: "Pod Security Admission enforces on Pods, not on the Deployment. The ReplicaSet's Pod creations are rejected; `kubectl describe rs` shows the violations." },
    ],
